Part 2: Backups - Protecting Your Data
Bucking Up data
A: We take backups daily by default, and you can customize the schedule.
What gets backed up:
•	Everything - All  KPIs, user accounts, performance reviews, department structures, everything
Backup schedule (default):
•	Full backup - Every night at 2 AM (complete copy of everything)
•	Incremental backups - Every hour (only changes since the last backup)
Simple explanation: A full backup is like taking a photograph of your entire house. An incremental backup is like only noting what moved since yesterday's photo - much faster and smaller.
Backups Times:
•	Daily backups: 30 days
•	Weekly backups: 3 months
•	Monthly backups: 1 year
What this means for you: If someone accidentally deletes important KPIs on Tuesday, we can restore them from Monday's backup. If you discover a problem in December that started in September, we can go back to a September backup.
Manually backup triggering
A: This is actually a best practice we recommend.
When to manually trigger a backup:
•	Before importing large amounts of data
•	Before making major configuration changes
•	Before running end-of-year reports
•	Before any system update
How Backup:
1.	Go to Config → Backups
2.	Click "Trigger Backup"
3.	Select which app to back up (or choose "All Apps")
4.	Click "Start"
The backup typically takes 1-5 minutes depending on how much data you have. Once complete, you'll see a confirmation.
Pro tip: Think of this like saving a game before a boss battle. You probably won't need it, but you'll be extremely grateful if something goes wrong.
Restoration
A: The system supports granular restore - you don't have to restore everything.
What you can restore individually:
•	A single KPI
•	One user's account
•	A department's data
•	A specific performance review
•	A mission status report
Example scenario: An employee accidentally deletes their KPIs. Instead of restoring everyone's data from last night (which might undo other people's work), you can restore just that employee's KPIs from yesterday's backup.
How it works: The backup system stores data by app and by record type. When you restore, you can select exactly what you need.
Simple analogy: This is like having a "undo" button that goes back days or weeks, not just a few seconds.
Disaster Recovery
Disaster Recovery (DR) - Config app handles it automatically.
If the entire system goes down:
Step 1 - Detection: Health monitors detect the failure within minutes
Step 2 - Notification: Administrators receive alerts via email and SMS
Step 3 - Recovery: We restore from the most recent backup
Step 4 - Validation: We verify everything is working correctly
Step 5 - Resumption: Normal operations continue
Recovery time targets:
•	RPO (Recovery Point Objective) - Maximum data loss: 1 hour
o	Simple meaning: You won't lose more than 1 hour of work
•	RTO (Recovery Time Objective) - Maximum downtime: 4 hours
o	Simple meaning: You'll be back up and running within 4 hours
Example: If the server crashes at 3 PM, the worst-case scenario is:
•	You lose data entered between 2 PM and 3 PM (1 hour max)
•	You're back online by 7 PM (4 hours later)
For most businesses, this is acceptable. For 24/7 operations, we can configure faster recovery (at higher cost).
Backup testing
We call this a "DR Drill" - it's like a fire drill for your data.
What a DR Drill does:
•	Uses a copy of your backup (not the live system)
•	Tests the entire restore process
•	Verifies data integrity
•	Measures how long recovery takes
•	Does NOT affect your live system or users
How often to run drills: Monthly is recommended. Quarterly is the minimum.
What you'll learn from a drill:
•	Your backups are actually usable (many companies never test and discover their backups are corrupt!)
•	How long recovery really takes (not just estimates)
•	If any steps in the recovery plan need improvement
Simple analogy: You test your building's fire alarm system even though you hope you never need it. DR drills are the same for your data.
