Part 3: Maintenance - Keeping the System Healthy
Q8: What is a "maintenance window"? When do we need one?
A: A maintenance window is a scheduled period when the system might be slow or unavailable for updates.
When maintenance is needed:
•	Software updates (new features, bug fixes)
•	Database optimization (keeping things fast)
•	Security patches
•	Hardware upgrades (for self-hosted customers)
Types of maintenance:
•	Full maintenance - System completely unavailable (rare, typically 1-2 hours)
•	Partial maintenance - Some features unavailable, others work normally
•	Rolling maintenance - No downtime, users might experience a brief pause
Example: "We need to update the KPI calculation engine. We'll do a partial maintenance window on Saturday from 2-4 AM. During this time, you can still view dashboards but can't enter new data."
Best practice: Always schedule maintenance during your lowest activity period. For most businesses, that's weekend nights (Saturday 2-6 AM).
Q9: Can we postpone maintenance if it conflicts with our busy season?
A: Absolutely. You have full control over maintenance scheduling.
What you can do:
•	Postpone - Push maintenance to a later date
•	Reschedule - Move to a different time that works better
•	Cancel - Skip this maintenance entirely (not recommended for security updates)
Blackout periods: You can configure "blackout dates" when maintenance is never allowed:
•	Month-end close (last 3 days of month)
•	Quarterly reporting week
•	End-of-year (Dec 15 - Jan 15)
•	Your company's annual retreat
How to set this up: Go to Config → Maintenance → Blackout Periods → Add dates
Real-world example: If you're a retail company, you'd block maintenance from Black Friday through Christmas. If you're an accounting firm, you'd block maintenance during tax season (Jan-April).
Q10: What happens during maintenance? Will users lose their work?
A: We design maintenance to be as non-disruptive as possible.
During full maintenance:
•	Users cannot log in
•	Active sessions are ended (with warning beforehand)
•	No data loss - Any unsaved work before maintenance starts is preserved
During partial maintenance:
•	Users can still access unaffected features
•	Example: KPI entry might be paused, but you can still view dashboards and run reports
Warning system:
•	7 days before: Administrators notified
•	24 hours before: All users see banner notification
•	1 hour before: Reminder notification
•	5 minutes before: Final warning with countdown
What about unsaved work? The system will warn users 15 minutes, 10 minutes, 5 minutes, and 1 minute before maintenance. If they still have unsaved work at the 1-minute mark, we recommend they save immediately.
Simple analogy: Maintenance is like your airline announcing a gate change. You have plenty of warning, and you won't miss your flight if you pay attention.
Q11: What is "risk-based maintenance"? Does the system decide when to fix things?
A: Risk-based maintenance means the system recommends maintenance based on how risky it is to wait.
What the system monitors:
•	Error rates - If KPIs are failing to calculate, that's high risk
•	Performance - If dashboards are loading slowly, that's medium risk
•	Security patches - Critical security updates are highest risk
Risk levels:
•	Low risk - Can wait for scheduled maintenance (e.g., cosmetic updates)
•	Medium risk - Should be addressed within 2 weeks (e.g., slow performance)
•	High risk - Address within 1 week (e.g., minor bugs)
•	Critical risk - Address immediately (e.g., security vulnerability)
What the system does:
•	Monitors continuously
•	Calculates risk scores
•	Suggests maintenance windows
•	Does NOT automatically start maintenance - administrators must approve
Example: The system notices that KPI calculations are taking 5 seconds instead of 0.5 seconds. It flags this as "medium risk" and suggests a maintenance window to optimize the database. You can approve, postpone, or ignore.
Q12: What is "conditional maintenance"?
A: Conditional maintenance means the system can automatically trigger maintenance when certain conditions are met.
Conditions you can configure:
•	Health check failure - If an app is unhealthy for 3 consecutive checks, trigger maintenance
•	Error rate threshold - If error rate exceeds 5% in an hour, trigger maintenance
•	Performance degradation - If response time doubles, trigger maintenance
What happens when triggered:
1.	System detects the condition
2.	Creates a maintenance window (usually "partial maintenance" for the affected app)
3.	Notifies administrators
4.	If auto-approve is enabled, starts maintenance immediately
Example: The KPI engine starts throwing errors. Within 5 minutes, the system creates a maintenance window, notifies you, and (if you've enabled auto-approve) restarts the service to fix the issue.
Important: This is optional. You can disable conditional maintenance if you prefer manual control. We recommend enabling it for non-critical issues and keeping manual control for major changes.
