Part 4: Disaster Recovery - When Things Go Wrong
Q13: What's the difference between a backup and disaster recovery?
A: This is a very common question. Think of it this way:
Concept	Analogy	Purpose
Backup	Having a spare tire	Fixes small problems (deleted file, corrupted data)
Disaster Recovery	Having a whole spare car	Fixes big problems (server died, building burned down)
Backup handles:
•	Accidental deletion
•	Corrupted data
•	User errors ("Oops, I deleted the wrong thing")
Disaster Recovery handles:
•	Complete server failure
•	Database corruption
•	Natural disaster affecting your data center
•	Ransomware attack
Simple explanation: Backups are for "I made a mistake." Disaster Recovery is for "the whole system is broken."
What you get with DR:
•	Ability to restore to a completely new server
•	Geographic redundancy (backups in different locations)
•	Documented recovery procedures
•	Regular testing (DR drills)
Q14: What is failover? What is failback?
A: These are DR terms for switching between systems.
Failover - Switching from your main (primary) system to a backup (standby) system
Failback - Switching back to your main system after the problem is fixed
Simple analogy:
•	Failover = Switching to your spare tire
•	Failback = Putting the regular tire back on after repair
How it works:
1.	Normal operation - Primary server is active, standby server is ready but idle
2.	Problem detected - Primary server fails or becomes slow
3.	Failover triggered - Traffic switches to standby server (typically 1-5 minutes)
4.	Operations continue - Users might notice a brief pause, but work continues
5.	Primary fixed - You repair the primary server
6.	Failback - Switch back to primary (usually during a maintenance window)
What this means for users: In most cases, they won't even know a failover happened. They might notice a 1-2 minute "loading" delay, but then everything works normally.
Q15: Can we practice disaster recovery without affecting our live system?
A: Yes! This is called a DR Drill or Tabletop Exercise.
Types of drills:
1. Tabletop Drill (no technical work):
•	Team walks through the recovery plan verbally
•	Discusses "what if" scenarios
•	Identifies gaps in the plan
•	Takes 1-2 hours
2. Technical Drill (uses copy of data):
•	Restores backup to a test environment
•	Actually performs the recovery steps
•	Measures how long it takes
•	Takes 4-8 hours
3. Full Simulation (as real as possible):
•	Actually fails over to standby
•	Runs on standby for 1-2 hours
•	Fails back to primary
•	Takes 8-12 hours (often on a weekend)
Recommendation: Do a tabletop drill quarterly and a technical drill annually. The cost of a drill is tiny compared to discovering your DR plan doesn't work during a real emergency.
Q16: What's the cost of not having DR? (The scary question)
A: Let's be direct about this.
Without DR:
•	Server dies at 3 PM on a Friday
•	You discover your backups are corrupt (they were never tested)
•	You spend the weekend trying to recover
•	By Monday, you've lost 3 months of KPI data
•	Your team re-enters Q3 data from printed reports (takes 2 weeks)
•	Some data is gone forever
•	Trust in the system is damaged
With DR:
•	Server dies at 3 PM on a Friday
•	DR system detects failure within 2 minutes
•	Failover to standby happens automatically
•	Users might notice a 2-minute pause
•	By 3:05 PM, everyone is working normally
•	Primary server is replaced over the weekend
•	Failback happens Sunday night
•	Monday morning, everything is normal
The business case: The cost of DR is typically 20-30% of your subscription. The cost of NOT having DR could be days of downtime, lost data, and lost trust. Most businesses decide the insurance is worth it.
