Part 5: Health Monitoring - Knowing Everything is OK
Q17: What does "health check" mean? What gets monitored?
A: Health checks are like vital signs for your system - they tell you if everything is working properly.
What we monitor:
Application Health (each app):
•	Is the app responding? (yes/no)
•	How fast does it respond? (response time)
•	Is it making database errors? (error rate)
System Health (infrastructure):
•	CPU usage (is the server working too hard?)
•	Memory usage (is it running out of RAM?)
•	Disk space (are we running out of storage?)
•	Database connections (are too many people trying to connect at once?)
Business Health (your data):
•	Are KPIs calculating correctly?
•	Are reports generating successfully?
•	Are scheduled jobs running on time?
Simple analogy: This is like a fitness tracker for your software. Just as a fitness tracker monitors your heart rate, steps, and sleep, health monitoring tracks your system's vital signs.
Q18: How will we know if something is wrong?
A: The system uses a traffic light system - green, yellow, red.
Color	Meaning	What happens
🟢 Green (Healthy)	Everything is working normally	Nothing - system is happy
🟡 Yellow (Degraded)	Something is slow or partially broken	Administrator gets email notification
🔴 Red (Unhealthy)	Something is broken	Administrator gets email + SMS, system may trigger conditional maintenance
Real-world example:
•	Green - Response time under 500ms, zero errors → "All good"
•	Yellow - Response time 2 seconds, occasional errors → "Something's slowing us down"
•	Red - Response time 10+ seconds, constant errors → "Something is broken, take action"
Notification options:
•	Email (default for all alerts)
•	SMS (for critical alerts only)
•	Slack/Teams webhook (if your company uses these)
•	Webhook (custom integration with your monitoring system)
You can configure: Which alerts go where, and who receives them. For example, you might send all alerts to your IT team, but only critical alerts to the IT manager's phone.
Q19: Can we add our own health checks for custom integrations?
A: Yes! The system supports custom health checks.
What you can monitor:
•	External API endpoints (e.g., your HR system)
•	Custom reports (e.g., "does the monthly board report generate?")
•	Integration health (e.g., "is the SSO connection working?")
How to add a custom check:
1.	Go to Config → Health → Custom Checks
2.	Give it a name (e.g., "HR System Sync")
3.	Provide the endpoint URL
4.	Set expected response (e.g., status code 200, contains "healthy")
5.	Choose how often to check (e.g., every 5 minutes)
Example: You have an integration that syncs users from your HR system every night. You can add a health check that confirms the sync completed successfully. If it fails, you'll know in the morning and can fix it before employees notice.
