Part 6: Schedules - Automating Everything
Q20: What kind of things can we schedule?
A: Almost any repetitive task can be scheduled.
Common scheduled tasks:
•	Daily backups - 2 AM every day
•	Weekly reports - Every Monday at 8 AM email department KPIs
•	Monthly maintenance - First Saturday at 2 AM
•	Quarterly DR drills - First Sunday of the quarter
•	Health checks - Every 5 minutes (automatic)
How scheduling works: You provide a cron expression (don't worry, we have helpers to generate these).
Example cron expressions:
•	0 2 * * * = Every day at 2 AM
•	0 9 * * 1 = Every Monday at 9 AM
•	0 0 1 * * = First day of every month at midnight
•	*/30 * * * * = Every 30 minutes
We provide a cron helper: You can select common patterns from a dropdown (e.g., "Daily at 2 AM", "Weekly on Sunday"), and the system generates the expression for you.
Q21: What happens if a scheduled task fails?
A: The system handles failures gracefully.
If a scheduled backup fails:
1.	The system retries up to 3 times (spaced 5 minutes apart)
2.	If all retries fail, the job is marked "failed"
3.	Administrators receive an alert
4.	The next scheduled backup runs as normal
If a scheduled maintenance fails:
1.	Maintenance is aborted (system returns to normal)
2.	Administrators receive critical alert
3.	A rollback plan is executed if needed
4.	The system logs detailed error information for debugging
If a scheduled report fails:
1.	The system logs the failure
2.	Administrators receive a warning (not critical)
3.	You can manually run the report later
Automatic retry: Most scheduled tasks retry automatically. You can configure how many retries and the delay between them.
Q22: Can we pause schedules (like during holidays)?
A: Yes! You can temporarily pause any schedule.
How to pause a schedule:
1.	Go to Config → Schedules
2.	Find the schedule you want to pause
3.	Click the toggle switch (On → Off)
4.	Choose an optional end date (when to automatically resume)
Example use cases:
•	Pause weekly reports during company shutdown (Dec 25 - Jan 1)
•	Pause backups during a major data migration
•	Pause maintenance schedules during your busy season
Resuming: Either manually turn the schedule back on, or set an auto-resume date when you pause it.
Pro tip: Use the "pause until" feature - you won't forget to turn schedules back on after the holidays.
