Part 8: Security & Access Control
Q25: Who can access the Config app? Can regular employees see backups?
A: No - the Config app is strictly limited to Super Admins and Client Admins.
Access restrictions:
•	Super Admin - Complete access (typically 1-2 people in your organization)
•	Client Admin - Can view dashboards, trigger backups, schedule maintenance
•	Everyone else - Cannot access Config app at all
What regular users see:
•	Nothing about backups or disaster recovery
•	No maintenance schedules
•	No health monitoring data
•	They might see a "maintenance mode" banner when the system is down, but nothing else
Why this matters: Regular employees don't need to know about backup schedules or DR plans. This reduces confusion and maintains security.
Can we give read-only access to auditors? Yes! We can create a special "Auditor" role that can view audit logs and backup history but cannot change anything.
Q26: Are backups encrypted? Can someone steal our backup files?
A: Yes, backups are encrypted at every stage.
Encryption at rest (stored backups):
•	Backups are encrypted using AES-256 (military-grade encryption)
•	Encryption keys are stored separately from the data
•	Even if someone stole the backup files, they couldn't read them without the key
Encryption in transit (moving backups):
•	Backups are encrypted while being uploaded to storage
•	TLS 1.3 encryption for all network transfers
Encryption keys:
•	You can use our keys (simpler) or provide your own (more control)
•	Keys can be rotated regularly (we recommend every 90 days)
•	Compromised keys can be revoked immediately
What this means: Your backup data is as secure as your live data - probably more secure, since backups are in a separate location with different access controls.
Q27: What is an audit log? How long do we keep audit logs?
A: The audit log is a complete record of every significant action in the Config app.
What gets logged:
•	Who triggered a backup (and when)
•	Who restored data (and what they restored)
•	Who scheduled or cancelled maintenance
•	Who changed backup policies
•	Who rotated encryption keys
•	Who viewed audit logs (meta-audit!)
For each action, we record:
•	User's email address
•	Action performed
•	Timestamp
•	IP address
•	Details of what changed
Retention:
•	Default: 365 days (1 year)
•	Configurable: 90 days to 7 years
•	Can be exported for external compliance
Why this matters: When something goes wrong, you can answer "who did what and when". For compliance (SOC2, ISO 27001), audit logs are mandatory.
Example: "Someone deleted last month's backups!" Check the audit log → See that Administrator John triggered a retention policy at 2 AM that deleted old backups. John can explain why (or apologize for the mistake).
